WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:01.179
Good morning John!

00:00:01.179 --> 00:00:05.299
A couple of weeks ago Tumblr changed how it
displays reblogs and I did not like this because

00:00:05.299 --> 00:00:07.919
it was change and I was frustrated.

00:00:07.919 --> 00:00:12.560
And then last week I went to Yellowstone National
Park, potentially the coolest place on earth.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.930
It was the first national park ever created
and it's one of the most dangerous things

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:16.930
on the planet.

00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:20.731
Not, like, to individuals travelling in the
park, though that can be dangerous too, I

00:00:20.731 --> 00:00:23.150
mean to the whole world, really.

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:27.680
Part of the reason Yellowstone is so interesting
is that it is a very, very big volcano. 3

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.619
times in the last 2 million years the Yellowstone
supervolcano has erupted, spewing out tens

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:38.850
or even hundreds of cubic miles of debris
affecting global weather patterns and laying

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.980
ash down as far away as Texas.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.800
Now, this isn't gonna happen again anytime
soon, but it will happen again someday, and

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:48.989
when it happens, the park will basically be
gone.

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:52.550
But the remarkable thing is
that it happened 600,000 years ago, and as

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:57.770
you travel the park, you drive over the rim
of the caldera formed in that eruption.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:01:02.130
And other than that, the desolation that eruption
caused is pretty much invisible.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:07.810
Life just rushed back in to fill the void
created by that massive event.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.200
The ground is a thing that doesn't move.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.859
If you're grounded, you're like a rock.

00:01:12.859 --> 00:01:13.859
People change.

00:01:13.859 --> 00:01:16.790
The things we make change, but the land remains
the same.

00:01:16.790 --> 00:01:18.170
And yet it doesn't.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.490
In Yellowstone, more than anywhere, places
do not stay the same.

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:23.990
Geysers turn on and off.

00:01:23.990 --> 00:01:26.490
New springs eat through the ground.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:31.030
As the water cools, crystals are deposited,
building into giant mounds.

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:37.780
And the ground itself rises and falls in ripples
and domes across the land as hot gas and magma

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.420
surges beneath the surface.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:44.830
Everything constant is a lie, and my life
continues to remind me of that.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:47.650
We want stability, but we can't have it.

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:49.540
And that's both sad and wonderful.

00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:52.590
Now, we apply false stability to Yellowstone.

00:01:52.590 --> 00:01:57.510
It's most famous landmark, of course, is Old
Faithful, geyser that blasts every 70 minutes

00:01:57.510 --> 00:02:02.370
or so with remarkable regularity, but Old
Faithful will one day cheat on us and everyone

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:03.380
knows that.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.710
Some day all of the benches and lodges we
built around it will just be a semi-circle

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.519
around a bunch of dry rocks.

00:02:09.519 --> 00:02:12.209
Here's New Blue Spring, now dormant and white.

00:02:12.209 --> 00:02:17.720
And this is Constant Geyser, so named because
it never stops spurting, until it did.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.909
But Yellowstone has broken no contract with
us.

00:02:20.909 --> 00:02:23.129
It makes no pretense of stability.

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:27.189
It's honest, in a way that the rest of the
Earth and even life isn't.

00:02:27.189 --> 00:02:30.909
An honesty that reminds me that the inevitability
of change, though it terrifies me, it also

00:02:30.909 --> 00:02:32.099
excites me.

00:02:32.099 --> 00:02:36.699
That incurable illness that always makes me
want to know what's next; to see the future,

00:02:36.699 --> 00:02:39.530
the future where things, are not like they
are today.

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.430
The future that will allow me to look back
and miss what once was.

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:47.379
That will scrub my present day of the dinginess,
of the crud, until I only remember the brilliant

00:02:47.379 --> 00:02:50.040
sparkles, and the deepest gashes.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:55.029
We're all standing on that cliff, beyond which
there is nothing, until there is.

00:02:55.029 --> 00:02:59.400
And we always believe that it will be there,
especially those of us that have been so rarely

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:05.760
burned, until someday it isn't there and your
life has changed; your world has changed.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.680
And there's nothing you can do to get it back
again.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:10.359
Yellowstone doesn't lie to me.

00:03:10.359 --> 00:03:14.669
Yellowstone reminds me that even something
as constant as the ground beneath my feet

00:03:14.669 --> 00:03:18.959
could just one day fly into the air and land
in Texas.

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:23.889
And so maybe the way my reblogs look on Tumblr
isn't the thing I should be worrying about.

00:03:23.889 --> 00:03:26.620
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:28.340
We've been in a really long traffic jam.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:31.090
We don't know what we've been in a traffic
jam for.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:34.650
And when this minivan moves, we will find
out.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.480
What is it?

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:37.559
Katherine: It's nothing.

00:03:37.559 --> 00:03:38.999
Hank: It's a bison.

00:03:38.999 --> 00:03:40.089
It's a bison.

00:03:40.089 --> 00:03:41.140
There it is.

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:42.140
So, bye.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:43.369
It was nice to meet you.

